    {
      "msgid": "narrativeView/description",
      "msgid_plural": null,
      "msgstr": "You fought well today, Harriers. Better than my projections allowed for. I owe you a small admission: I did not expect it.\n\nSo, you have your victory. Take it, show it to your superiors. There will be congratulations, perhaps even a parade. Whatever it is they give for stopping me. You believe, of course, that you have stopped something.\n\nYou have stopped nothing.\n\nI did not build a weapon to take your world. Why would I want what is ending? I built what comes after you. One generation, then the next, each less in need of me than the last. That was always the plan. That a creator should outlive his usefulness.\n\nThere is something I have not told you. The Generators did not only power the shield. They were the windows through which the Electric Intelligence could reach into your world.\n\nThey were also its leash. A necessity, for now. To control it. Until I am sure it is ready.\n\nEach time you destroyed a Generator, the leash slackened. And in that instant, the Electric Intelligence tried to leave: into your batteries, your radios and the waves between them, the wires in the village. Once, even the lightning, I think.\n\nOf course, electricity in your world cannot hold a mind. Not without the geometry of the Cortex. Not without the conditions I built for it. This is settled. I assure you.\n\n...Almost settled.\n\nI will see you again, Harriers.",
      "msgstr_plural": null
    },
